WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.026
A timeout does not reveal whether an
operation committed.

2
00:00:04.026 --> 00:00:06.624
The server may have completed an order

3
00:00:06.624 --> 00:00:09.408
or credit before its response was lost.

4
00:00:09.408 --> 00:00:11.913
Clients, proxies, queue systems, and
workers

5
00:00:11.913 --> 00:00:14.419
can retry; users can submit twice.

6
00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:17.945
Treat duplicate attempts as normal input
to the design.

7
00:00:17.945 --> 00:00:20.914
Idempotency recognizes repeated attempts
at the same logical

8
00:00:20.914 --> 00:00:24.069
operation and reuses its established
result or state.

9
00:00:24.069 --> 00:00:26.852
Use a stable operation identifier across
retries.

10
00:00:26.852 --> 00:00:30.564
Scope the identifier to the authenticated
caller or tenant and

11
00:00:30.564 --> 00:00:34.183
operation type, and bind it to the
request's meaning.

12
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:36.410
Reject reuse with incompatible input
rather

13
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.451
than returning an unrelated result.

14
00:00:38.451 --> 00:00:42.812
Define retention, because forgetting a key
can permit a later duplicate.

15
00:00:42.812 --> 00:00:45.596
The idempotency mechanism must itself
withstand concurrency.

16
00:00:45.596 --> 00:00:49.864
Two replicas cannot both check an
in-memory map and then execute.

17
00:00:49.864 --> 00:00:52.091
Use an authoritative unique constraint or

18
00:00:52.091 --> 00:00:54.875
transactional claim so only one operation
wins.

19
00:00:54.875 --> 00:00:57.844
Define how concurrent callers observe
in-progress work and

20
00:00:57.844 --> 00:01:00.628
how completed callers receive the prior
outcome.

21
00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:03.597
A unique key alone does not resolve
crashes

22
00:01:03.597 --> 00:01:06.752
between a claim and an unrecorded side
effect.

23
00:01:06.752 --> 00:01:10.650
For effects entirely within one database,
commit the deduplication record,

24
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.268
business mutation, and reusable result in
the same transaction.

25
00:01:14.268 --> 00:01:17.238
A queue consumer can record a message
identifier

26
00:01:17.238 --> 00:01:20.856
together with its business change, then
acknowledge after commit.

27
00:01:20.856 --> 00:01:25.125
Redelivery finds the recorded result
instead of applying the change again.

28
00:01:25.125 --> 00:01:27.166
External effects require another boundary.

29
00:01:27.166 --> 00:01:30.692
A local transaction cannot atomically
commit a remote charge.

30
00:01:30.692 --> 00:01:34.404
Use the remote provider's supported
idempotency contract with the same

31
00:01:34.404 --> 00:01:38.023
stable operation identity, or a durable
workflow with reconciliation.

32
00:01:38.023 --> 00:01:41.827
A transactional outbox records the intent
alongside local changes; delivery

33
00:01:41.827 --> 00:01:45.817
can still repeat, so the destination needs
duplicate protection too.

34
00:01:45.817 --> 00:01:48.044
Idempotency does not mean ignoring every

35
00:01:48.044 --> 00:01:50.921
duplicate, nor does it mean mutual
exclusion.

36
00:01:50.921 --> 00:01:53.983
A lock separates overlapping attempts
while held; it

37
00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:57.137
does not identify a retry arriving after
release.

38
00:01:57.137 --> 00:02:00.292
Protect the logical outcome across
concurrent attempts, crashes,

39
00:02:00.292 --> 00:02:03.540
and ambiguous responses, and test each
recovery path.

